00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:11.767
[SPEAKING ITALIAN]

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:15.920
That's as much Italian
that I can speak.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.040
Thank you for joining me for
this talk this afternoon.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.340
With the looming weather
that is coming up,

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:26.180
I really appreciate that you
made your way here to my talk.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.740
What am I going to
talk about today

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:32.210
is Google Cloud Spanner, a
horizontally global distributed

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:33.410
relational database.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.500
And I'm going to talk a
little bit in more detail how

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:37.770
everything works.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:41.450
But before that, I want
to put a couple of words

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:44.790
to my person in terms of like
what my job at Google is.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:47.120
I'm a developer
advocate at Google.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.760
And we understand
our job as being

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.700
a bi-directional
interface, so to say,

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.310
between developers that
build on our products

00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.200
and the developers
within Google that

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.070
build the products for you.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:04.310
So please, feel free to reach
out to me on Twitter or GitHub

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:07.130
if you have
constructive feedback,

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.490
if you have some ideas what you
want to do with Google Cloud

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:11.960
platform or certain products.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:17.200
And we are happy to be
there for you to respond to.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.040
Now, what am I going to cover
today, in this talk is I

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.980
want to give a brief
history about how Spanner

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:28.780
was built within Google
and why it was built.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.160
I'm going to go
a little bit more

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.090
in detail about what is the
added feature set in terms

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:36.940
of Cloud Spanner, like what
are adding to the version

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:38.410
that we have, internally?

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:40.090
And then I will
spend a fair amount

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:46.130
on explaining the inner workings
of Spanner and give a demo.

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:49.080
Now, why did we build Spanner?

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.410
Let's go back about 12 years.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:53.480
And Google has
been at that point

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.490
running on top of
a charted MySQL.

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.200
So we had a database,
which was charted

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:58.820
based on the customer ID.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.800
Our [? S ?] business
was running through it.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.350
And there are a lot of millions
of dollars, basically, backed

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.720
on this charted MySQL system.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.070
Now, since we were
growing really fast,

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:12.710
we had to basically rechart
that database multiple times.

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:16.400
And the last time we
recharted that database,

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:19.880
it took over two years
to rechart that database.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:22.010
So clearly, this
was not a solution

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:25.130
that we could go forward
in the terms of scalability

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.060
that we needed.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:29.690
So what did we really
need from a database

00:02:29.690 --> 00:02:32.600
where lots of
customers are basically

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.250
betting their money on?

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:37.460
So we had customers
with budgets.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:40.970
We had our own systems
that needed, basically,

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:44.930
transactional consistency
as a back end.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.450
So we needed a database
which scales horizontally,

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.870
which supports
ACID transactions,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:55.310
and most importantly, that can
support no downtime, updates

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:58.250
of security updates,
and schema updates

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:00.410
and things like that
because every time,

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.830
we basically had to
shut down our back end,

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:06.140
it would be a loss of money for
us and lots of our customers,

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:07.110
as well.

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:09.110
So this was not acceptable.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:12.100
Also, because we were
dealing with people's money,

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:16.880
eventual consistency was not
acceptable for us either.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.710
So fast forward
about 10, 12 years

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:22.790
that we were running Cloud
Spanner inside of Google.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.920
We made it now
available for all of you

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.730
to basically use it
with your applications.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.160
But we added a couple
of things which we think

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.350
are really important
for Cloud Solution

00:03:33.350 --> 00:03:35.450
to offer that under the
portfolio of Google Cloud

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:36.750
platform.

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.590
So one of the very first
things is Google Cloud Spanner

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:41.810
comes as a fully
managed service.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.230
It is the same back end,
the same engineering,

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:49.070
the same technology that we
use internally for our Spanner.

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:52.310
But you get it, basically,
through a UI, fully managed,

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.560
very easy to set up, and
then, within seconds,

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:56.250
available for you.

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:59.270
It's still the same performance
and global consistency

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:01.520
that we have inside.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.990
Also, what is
really important is

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:05.930
we wanted to meet people
and developers where

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.780
they are in terms of
familiarity with interacting

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.680
with relational databases.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.029
So Spanner supports the familiar
SQL and the 2011 standard

00:04:16.029 --> 00:04:21.500
that has traditional relational
semantics and ACID semantics.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:23.930
Also, Spanner supports
an automatic [? V ?]

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:29.100
synchronous replication for
regional and multiregional

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:29.660
setups.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.640
And it is battle
tested within Google

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:33.560
for more than five years.

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:35.990
Now, for regional setups,
we provide, actually,

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.190
four nines availability, which
boils down to 4 minutes and 38

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.650
seconds, roughly,
of unavailability

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:45.140
that we accept for
a regional setup.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.140
Later this year, we will
support a multiregional setup

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:50.180
that can span the
globe, where you

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:53.990
get five nines of availability
for that setup, which basically

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:58.430
boils down to about 26 seconds
per month in unavailability

00:04:58.430 --> 00:05:01.530
that we guarantee.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.080
Now, another thing which
was really important for us

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:07.590
was when we were working with
our early access customers

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.680
to help them integrate
Cloud Spanner

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.400
into their applications.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.040
So we were looking
at this like, OK,

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:16.380
how can we make it
as easy as possible

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.400
to adapt Cloud Spanner?

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:22.370
One I already mentioned was
SQL, that we support SQL

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.700
and that we support one SQL
standard across our product

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:26.550
portfolio.

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:30.170
So there has been, in the
past, multiple SQL dialects.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:35.580
We now switched to SQL ANSI
2011 across our portfolio.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:41.560
So BigQuery, as also
Spanner, supports ANSI 2011.

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.135
For all of our data that
is stored in Spanner,

00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:48.050
it is encrypted at
rest and in flight.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:51.500
And we added things like
audit locking and integration

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.930
of our IAM, so our Identity
and Access Management,

00:05:54.930 --> 00:06:00.155
so that you can control
access to data within Spanner.

00:06:00.155 --> 00:06:04.450
With the launch, we provide,
also, the client library system

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:05.380
in four languages.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.985
And we are working hard on
more languages to add to this.

00:06:08.985 --> 00:06:13.250
So currently, we have Java,
Go, Python, and Node.js

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:16.450
as the languages that you can
use today, out of the box.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:19.780
We also have a REST API
and a [? GOPC ?] step

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:21.730
if you want to build
your own client libraries

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:23.440
in a different language.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.960
And we provide a
read-only JDBC driver

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:29.060
to integrate with
things like BI tools

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:32.200
from partners and
things like that.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.850
So how does Spanner compare
to a traditional database

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:39.720
or a non-SQL scalable database?

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.880
If you look at Spanner,
in one sentence,

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:44.870
I would describe it
as you get relation--

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.730
like a database
with the semantics

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:51.350
of a relational database with
the scalability of a NoSQL

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:52.600
database.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:54.390
So what does that mean?

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:58.380
Spanner provides you things like
a strong schema, strong type

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:02.310
schema, provides you
SQL, which a lot of you

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:03.730
probably are familiar with.

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:05.940
It provides you
strong consistency.

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:09.035
And the way that Spanner
works behind the scenes,

00:07:09.035 --> 00:07:13.080
adds all the things like high
availability and horizontal

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:14.460
scalability.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:18.630
All the data is automatically
replicated to read replicas.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:21.590
And also, I will
go into more detail

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:23.690
in terms of the
architecture, like how

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:27.670
we achieve the high availability
and the horizontal scalability.

00:07:27.670 --> 00:07:32.850
Now, some might say, OK,
some-- many of these things

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.430
I can get from a traditional
relational database as well.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:38.520
So, yes, but many scenarios.

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:41.290
If you look at
scalability, for instance,

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:44.350
you look at a scenario
where you, for instance,

00:07:44.350 --> 00:07:47.820
have a charted MySQL
or charted Postgres.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.270
Now, if you want to
get scalability there,

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.020
there are certain
things that you can do.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:54.000
For once, you can,
for instance, add

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:57.930
some read replicas to offload
some workload from your master,

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:01.750
and by that, scale more for
your rights on your master.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:03.990
But you basically have
to hope that your cloud

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.410
provider or your
machine producer

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:10.620
gives you a bigger machine
as your system is growing,

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.450
as your business is growing.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.700
So you kind of run out
of head space eventually,

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:17.250
at some point, in the
terms of the amount of data

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:20.910
that you can manage with a
traditional relational database

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:22.770
or the amount of reads
and writes that you

00:08:22.770 --> 00:08:24.930
can handle with that system.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:30.100
Now, also, oftentimes, if you
go into the charted world off of

00:08:30.100 --> 00:08:33.419
or Postgres, you lose a lot of
the qualities of the underlying

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:34.440
database system.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.210
And a lot of the
semantics, you have

00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:38.909
to put up into your
business logic, which makes

00:08:38.909 --> 00:08:41.022
the whole system very complex.

00:08:41.022 --> 00:08:42.480
So now, with Spanner,
you basically

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:44.694
can put this logic
back into the database.

00:08:44.694 --> 00:08:46.860
And you don't have to care
in your application layer

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:48.580
about it.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:51.910
Now, comparing to
a non-SQL database,

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:54.630
you basically don't
have these things

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.800
where you have tweakable
consistency, where

00:08:56.800 --> 00:08:59.700
you might have to deal
with eventual consistency

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:03.170
for a Cassandra or MongoDB
or things like that.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.790
So we basically have
given the highest standard

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:09.250
of serializability with Spanner,
external serializability.

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:10.960
And there's no way around that.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:13.270
That's basically what you
get and you'll always have.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:18.420
Now, if we look at
the typical workloads

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:23.710
where Spanner is used, I want
to emphasize on two areas

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:26.050
where Spanner is really strong.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:30.610
One is, I mentioned, no
downtime, schema and security

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:33.190
updates behind the
scenes, basically,

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:37.730
for Spanner, which makes Spanner
a very high available system.

00:09:37.730 --> 00:09:41.160
So if you have a system
which is mission critical,

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.690
where you cannot accept
any downtime, Spanner--

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:47.590
even if you have not a lot of
data, Spanner is your choice.

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:50.320
That's something where
you should look into.

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:54.070
Another thing is in terms of
scalability for read/writes

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.490
and data size.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.490
So if you want to
scale horizontally

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:01.100
for your reads and
writes in transactions,

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:05.290
Spanner is, again, a very
good solution for this.

00:10:05.290 --> 00:10:08.530
You won't get the very
low latency query times

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:12.310
you might get with some of the
vertical scalable solutions.

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:17.030
But in the end, if you
design your database well

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:19.370
from a schema and
query standpoint,

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:21.580
you can basically,
with adding nodes,

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:24.010
scale very well horizontally.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.500
And you don't get the
hockey stick at the end

00:10:26.500 --> 00:10:30.390
if you get out of
capacity on your nodes.

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:33.210
So things where we
see a lot of usage

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.370
for these kinds of
workloads is, for instance,

00:10:35.370 --> 00:10:38.100
like financial system,
inventory management,

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:41.790
where you need kind of like
a global consistent database,

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:45.230
and things where you need
a lot of reads and writes.

00:10:45.230 --> 00:10:47.860
For a matter of fact, if
you look at the global data

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:50.240
plane, Google Cloud
platform, everything

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:53.150
in terms of the management
of resources and APIs

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:56.420
and virtual machines
is backed by Spanner

00:10:56.420 --> 00:10:58.490
as a global data plane.

00:10:58.490 --> 00:11:01.850
Another use case that
we see and that you

00:11:01.850 --> 00:11:04.100
can use Spanner for since
it's scalable in terms

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:06.290
of the size of
data it can handle

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:08.310
is database consolidation.

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:11.180
So instead of running
your daily ATL

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:16.460
drops of extracting them
data from a No-SQL solution,

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:18.460
and then extracting
some account data

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.800
from your relational
database, and then running

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:24.110
some analytics on
that, you can basically

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:27.430
think of putting all this
data into one database

00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:31.900
without the hassle of
having ATLs everywhere.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:33.670
So with that, I
want to look into,

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:37.050
how does Spanner actually
achieve these things

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:37.960
that I just claimed?

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:40.510
I claimed horizontal
scalability.

00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:43.300
I claimed that it can
grow in database size

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.850
up to terabytes and petabytes.

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:50.380
And I claimed that it can handle
a lot of read and write loads.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.180
So the way that
Spanner is doing this

00:11:52.180 --> 00:11:56.030
is a separation of
compute versus storage.

00:11:56.030 --> 00:11:59.980
So if you look here, this is
an example of a regional setup.

00:11:59.980 --> 00:12:01.660
So I have a regional instance.

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:05.560
I create that regional instance
in one of my preferred regions.

00:12:05.560 --> 00:12:08.830
And when I start
and buy three nodes,

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:11.960
I get, actually, behind
the scenes, nine nodes.

00:12:11.960 --> 00:12:13.480
So all the three
nodes that I buy

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:18.200
are replicated three
times in three zones.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:20.710
Now, in each of the zones,
I have storage, basically,

00:12:20.710 --> 00:12:24.130
so the storage is
replicated as well.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:26.940
Now, if you look at the
terminology in terms

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:32.550
of what are the visible
items for Spanner database

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:36.630
and instance from a client side
and what is behind the scenes

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:39.030
to enable the
horizontal scalability,

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.260
we start with an instance
that we just saw.

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.260
So you basically set up
your regional instant

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:46.500
that defines how many
nodes you have, how many--

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.380
where this region,
instance lives.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.990
Or later in this year, we
have a multiregional instance.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:55.650
So you can decide where your
multiregional instance lives.

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:59.520
In each of these instances, you
can create multiple databases,

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:01.500
up to 100 databases.

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:04.840
And then in each database,
you create tables,

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:08.790
as we are familiar with from a
MySQL database, for instance.

00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:11.280
Now, these tables are--
behind the scenes,

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.890
we split these
tables by key ranges.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:20.530
And the splits are done based
on the primary key and by range.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.510
Now, what enables
this splitting?

00:13:23.510 --> 00:13:26.700
The splitting enables us
to horizontal the scale.

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:28.440
So how do we do this?

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:30.900
Basically, the computer
sources that we

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:33.330
have available in
our regional instance

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:36.070
are responsible for
multiple splits.

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:39.750
So the splits are basically
parts of data in your database.

00:13:39.750 --> 00:13:43.170
Now, basically, a split
is part of a table.

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:46.500
Now, we see that in
all of these regions,

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:49.560
we basically have a
compute node or computer

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:54.610
source which is responsible for
split one here in this example.

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:58.560
And we have a Paxos group formed
by these three compute nodes

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:00.960
that are responsible
for the split one.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:05.844
Now, we use Paxos to select
a leader in this group.

00:14:05.844 --> 00:14:07.260
And now, once we
have this leader,

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:11.020
we can make sure
that only one compute

00:14:11.020 --> 00:14:14.220
node is responsible for--
and gating, basically,

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:17.730
writes write transaction
on that data piece.

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:20.520
By that, we can make sure that
we have global consistency

00:14:20.520 --> 00:14:24.060
because there's never two
leaders for a database.

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:27.520
Now, you could imagine,
in a distributed world,

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:29.880
this would incur a
lot of communication

00:14:29.880 --> 00:14:34.170
to make sure that we have, at
most, one responsible compute

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:37.020
node for a data piece.

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:39.930
The way that we remove some
of this communication overhead

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:45.030
is with an innovation that we
specifically did for Spanner.

00:14:45.030 --> 00:14:47.340
And it is called TrueTime.

00:14:47.340 --> 00:14:49.660
Now, what is TrueTime?

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.940
Imagine you're sitting
next to your neighbor,

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:54.370
and you're trying to
synchronize your watches.

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:58.030
Now, you go off and meet
again in a couple of hours.

00:14:58.030 --> 00:15:01.340
You have been exposed to
different temperatures,

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:05.760
different [? ionospheric ?]
rays and things like that.

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.750
Your clocks will drift
away from each other.

00:15:08.750 --> 00:15:11.950
So it's really hard, actually,
to synchronize clocks,

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:15.370
even in a small
vicinity of a room.

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:18.490
Now, think of synchronizing
clocks globally

00:15:18.490 --> 00:15:20.950
across data centers.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:23.070
Now, TrueTime
actually achieves that

00:15:23.070 --> 00:15:28.600
by a very smart technology
and semantic way.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:30.240
What TrueTime does
is, actually, we

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:34.920
use GPS to synchronize our
data centers clockwise.

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.780
And we use atomic
clocks in these data

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:40.640
centers to basically keep
a very accurate time.

00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:44.390
Now, still, we cannot use just
a single timestamp to make sure

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:47.000
that this timestamp is the
exact timestamp everywhere

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:48.090
in the world.

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:52.220
We have to quantify an interval,
which basically gives us

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:55.070
the uncertainty
of this timestamp.

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:56.330
What does that mean?

00:15:56.330 --> 00:15:57.710
A lot of words.

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:01.550
It basically means we get,
with TrueTime, a lower bound

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:03.980
and an upper bound
of a timestamp.

00:16:03.980 --> 00:16:05.570
The lower bound
of the timestamps

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:09.830
tells us that this timestamp has
passed everywhere in the world.

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:11.930
The upper bound of
that interval gives us

00:16:11.930 --> 00:16:15.650
a timestamp which tells us this
timestamp definitely hasn't

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:18.250
passed anywhere in the world.

00:16:18.250 --> 00:16:19.920
Now, with these
two timestamps, we

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.430
can make sure that there's
only one leader, exactly

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:25.230
one leader for a database.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:29.340
And by that, we get the
external serializability

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:33.220
for our transactions
that we provide.

00:16:33.220 --> 00:16:39.260
Now, in terms of the interval,
how long is that, roughly?

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:41.190
In our paper, we
published that it's

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:45.570
in the single-digit milliseconds
interval in terms of the lower

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:47.730
bound and the upper bound.

00:16:47.730 --> 00:16:51.780
Now, what happens if, for
instance, in my data center,

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:55.290
all atomic clocks fail
and all GPS fails?

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:57.540
What happens in this
case is that we actually

00:16:57.540 --> 00:17:00.840
go to a different data
center to get this TrueTime.

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:04.230
And we will calculate in
the travel time of the data,

00:17:04.230 --> 00:17:07.440
basically from our data center
to the next data center.

00:17:07.440 --> 00:17:10.710
Now, with that, the
interval basically widens,

00:17:10.710 --> 00:17:12.990
and the database slows down.

00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:15.420
But the database doesn't stop.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:16.619
So that is really important.

00:17:16.619 --> 00:17:19.680
The database, even in the case
that we lose atomic clocks

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.380
or we lose GPS in a data
center, the database

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:24.810
will continue working
and will continue

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:29.390
working in a very consistent--
global consistent rate.

00:17:29.390 --> 00:17:33.940
So let's demonstrate
that on a query.

00:17:33.940 --> 00:17:37.700
Here in this example, I
select from my event table

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:42.590
all the events that have
the name Next Milan.

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:45.080
So in a consistent
read scenario,

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:47.750
I basically send the
request from my client.

00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:52.010
And in this case, it comes
in through my read replica.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:54.410
And the read replica will go
to the leader for that data

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:58.490
piece in my Paxos group
and will ask, hey,

00:17:58.490 --> 00:18:00.407
do I have the latest data?

00:18:00.407 --> 00:18:02.240
It basically sends the
timestamp of the data

00:18:02.240 --> 00:18:04.880
that the read
replica already has.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:07.850
Now, the leader will respond,
yes, you have the latest data.

00:18:07.850 --> 00:18:11.920
And the data will be served
from my read replica.

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:17.710
In the case that the read
replica doesn't have the most

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:21.490
recent data, the leader
will just respond, no,

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:22.570
you don't have the data.

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:26.530
Just wait until timestamp, and
once you see that timestamp,

00:18:26.530 --> 00:18:28.520
you can respond to data.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:31.930
The reason is that the
data is already in flight

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.060
when this question
is basically asked.

00:18:34.060 --> 00:18:36.430
So we don't need to
send the data back,

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:39.340
even if the read replica
doesn't have the latest data.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:41.350
So we basically just
wait out that time.

00:18:41.350 --> 00:18:43.300
We basically wait
until that timestamp,

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:46.210
our upper bound
timestamp in TrueTime,

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:49.060
wait out that time that
we got from the leader.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:51.805
And once we have that
timestamp, and we have the data,

00:18:51.805 --> 00:18:55.880
we can basically respond.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:59.840
Now, we also support something
called stale or inconsistent

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:02.070
reads, time bound reads.

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:04.500
When are these useful?

00:19:04.500 --> 00:19:07.410
Imagine you have a
multiregional setup.

00:19:07.410 --> 00:19:11.060
And this is globally distributed
between the US, Europe,

00:19:11.060 --> 00:19:13.080
and Asia.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:17.420
Now, time bounded queries
gives you the possibility

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:21.110
to basically read data that
is a little bit older in a way

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:25.910
that you say, OK, I accept data
that is up to 15 seconds old.

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:28.520
Now, in that case,
the read replica

00:19:28.520 --> 00:19:31.880
can check, do I have data that
is, at most, 15 seconds old?

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:34.040
And if that is the
case, it basically

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:37.100
can respond immediately
without consulting

00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:39.020
the leader of that
data piece, which

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:42.950
could go across the
ocean in some cases

00:19:42.950 --> 00:19:47.450
to check if that data is
actually the most recent one.

00:19:47.450 --> 00:19:50.510
Now, what happens in the
read/write transaction?

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:53.630
In a read/write transaction,
when I start that transaction,

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:57.080
and I basically do-- within the
read/write transaction context,

00:19:57.080 --> 00:20:01.620
I do a query, I'm directly
routed to the leader of that

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:03.440
Paxos group of that for--

00:20:03.440 --> 00:20:05.840
which is responsible
for that data piece.

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:10.480
Now, the leader will acquire
locks, return the results.

00:20:10.480 --> 00:20:12.960
I do some modification on that.

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:15.120
And then I'm sending,
basically, the modifications

00:20:15.120 --> 00:20:16.380
to the leader.

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:19.230
And the leader will send
out the modification

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:22.040
to the group, the Paxos group.

00:20:22.040 --> 00:20:25.970
Once the leader receives a
majority of acknowledgement

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:29.720
from all the participants
in that group,

00:20:29.720 --> 00:20:32.720
it considers this
transaction committed,

00:20:32.720 --> 00:20:37.010
we'll basically release the
locks and return to the client

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:39.245
a success of that transaction.

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:46.510
If you look at the data
layout, which Spanner supports,

00:20:46.510 --> 00:20:50.800
I mentioned that it supports
traditional SQL and CSQL

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:53.650
in the same way as
other databases.

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:56.160
So we also have the
relational model

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.860
set you're familiar with,
with strongly typed schemas,

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:02.290
relational schemas,
so you see here,

00:21:02.290 --> 00:21:05.800
creating a singles table and
an album table with an ID

00:21:05.800 --> 00:21:08.770
and some single name
and an album ID.

00:21:08.770 --> 00:21:12.000
Now, if you look at
databases like MySQL,

00:21:12.000 --> 00:21:14.620
you have things like
foreign key constraints

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:18.160
or referential integrity that
you can get through that.

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:21.950
We don't support that in
the distributed system.

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:24.650
But we have something
which is basically

00:21:24.650 --> 00:21:27.430
a foreign key
constraint-- minus, minus.

00:21:27.430 --> 00:21:29.380
And that is called
interleaved tables.

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:31.510
What are interleaved tables?

00:21:31.510 --> 00:21:35.800
Now, interleaved tables give
you co-located data locality

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:38.860
in the sense of, here,
I have my singers table,

00:21:38.860 --> 00:21:42.070
and I have my album table, and
I interleave that album table

00:21:42.070 --> 00:21:43.930
into my singers table.

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:45.850
Now, all this data
is basically put

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:47.810
in one split that
I talked about,

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:49.870
which is a part of that table.

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:52.900
It will be put all
in one split per row.

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:55.360
So you have the
Beatles, for an example.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:57.970
And every album for
the Beatles will

00:21:57.970 --> 00:22:01.210
be living in the same
split as the row, the--

00:22:01.210 --> 00:22:03.049
where the Beatles lives in.

00:22:03.049 --> 00:22:04.715
And that gives you
kind of like a bridge

00:22:04.715 --> 00:22:07.870
or in terms of
like that joins can

00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:11.530
be answered very quickly
from one compute node that

00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:16.450
is controlling and
responsible for that split.

00:22:16.450 --> 00:22:19.390
So if we do this for our data
models that we have just seen,

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:22.990
we basically just
add an interleave in.

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:26.890
And we have to make sure that
the interleaved table shares

00:22:26.890 --> 00:22:30.280
the primary keys
of the outer table.

00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:32.920
And then we can basically
interleave the table.

00:22:32.920 --> 00:22:34.450
Also, what is really
important here

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:37.910
is that we support no
downtime schema updates.

00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:39.530
So you can add columns.

00:22:39.530 --> 00:22:43.960
You can change size
attributes of columns.

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:47.830
So it can go from a string 20 to
a string 50, things like that.

00:22:47.830 --> 00:22:53.410
All these schema updates run,
actually, in a transaction.

00:22:53.410 --> 00:22:55.690
So there are a couple
of things that you

00:22:55.690 --> 00:22:58.600
have to be aware
of when you migrate

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:02.740
from a traditional MySQL
database or Postgres or any

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:06.880
of the major, bigger database
vendors to Cloud Spanner

00:23:06.880 --> 00:23:11.210
to keep the scalability.

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:14.830
Now, one of the things is
that like our heavily used

00:23:14.830 --> 00:23:17.450
or that, many times, are used
in a traditional database

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:21.410
are things like outer and
command ID as a primary key.

00:23:21.410 --> 00:23:25.340
Now, this is actually
poison for Spanner in terms

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:28.100
of the horizontal scalability.

00:23:28.100 --> 00:23:31.490
And I will get into a little
bit more detail about that.

00:23:31.490 --> 00:23:33.890
So don't use any
monotonic increasing

00:23:33.890 --> 00:23:35.750
or decreasing primary keys.

00:23:35.750 --> 00:23:40.190
Don't use any timestamps as
primary keys in your schema.

00:23:40.190 --> 00:23:44.510
Also, be careful in terms of
how much you use interleaving.

00:23:44.510 --> 00:23:46.940
I mentioned all of
this lives, basically,

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:49.430
in the split of the
parent table, which

00:23:49.430 --> 00:23:53.150
means if you interleave
huge tables into one

00:23:53.150 --> 00:23:55.700
row of your parent
table, you also run out

00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:59.360
of capacity in terms of
resources for that node that

00:23:59.360 --> 00:24:02.340
is handling that split.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:04.860
So if you look at
using, for instance,

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:09.300
timestamps for a primary key,
what you would end up with

00:24:09.300 --> 00:24:12.660
is hotspotting one
of your compute nodes

00:24:12.660 --> 00:24:14.670
in managing that data.

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:18.240
So imagine you're
loading your database

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:21.870
and you're putting the
timestamp as the primary key.

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:24.630
What you end up with is
that all your new data

00:24:24.630 --> 00:24:27.690
that you load into the
database ends in the last split

00:24:27.690 --> 00:24:28.860
of your table.

00:24:28.860 --> 00:24:34.440
And the last split is handled
by one computer source.

00:24:34.440 --> 00:24:36.420
Now, to avoid this,
you can do things

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:43.590
like using new IDs, version 4,
or you're using charting IDs.

00:24:43.590 --> 00:24:45.090
So what does that look like?

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:49.680
If you look-- and everything
is also on our documentation.

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:51.840
You'll find more
detailed explanation

00:24:51.840 --> 00:24:56.010
about how you can optimize
your schemas and your workloads

00:24:56.010 --> 00:25:00.760
to not bottleneck on
hotspot on certain things.

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:03.210
Now, if you, for instance,
use a charting ID,

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:09.420
what it will look like is we
now prefix our primary key

00:25:09.420 --> 00:25:10.620
with a charting ID.

00:25:10.620 --> 00:25:14.160
And by that, we enable to
spread out the workload

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:19.260
across multiple compute
resources, as you can see here.

00:25:19.260 --> 00:25:23.534
With this, I want to
go in a little demo.

00:25:23.534 --> 00:25:24.200
Are we switched?

00:25:24.200 --> 00:25:26.330
Perfect.

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:30.650
So your first stop point, if you
want to learn more about Cloud

00:25:30.650 --> 00:25:35.330
Spanner and if you want to
get your hands on the Spanner,

00:25:35.330 --> 00:25:40.010
is cloud.google.com/spanner,
where you'll find a multitude

00:25:40.010 --> 00:25:43.220
of resources around
documentation, how to use it,

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:46.860
but also things
like case studies.

00:25:46.860 --> 00:25:49.310
As you can see down here,
we have a case study

00:25:49.310 --> 00:25:54.470
with [? Quizlet ?] that moved
and migrated from charted MySQL

00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:57.470
to Spanner and does
a lot of analytics

00:25:57.470 --> 00:26:01.460
around what did we have to
change in our applications

00:26:01.460 --> 00:26:04.430
to do this migration?

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:08.330
I'm also asked quite a couple
of times today already,

00:26:08.330 --> 00:26:11.700
does Spanner defeat
the CAP Theorem?

00:26:11.700 --> 00:26:13.980
Spanner does not
defeat the CAP Theorem.

00:26:13.980 --> 00:26:18.840
And you can read in detail
around a take of the inventor

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:21.990
of the CAP Theorem, Eric
Brewer, a take on that in terms

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:24.600
of like in contrast to Spanner.

00:26:24.600 --> 00:26:29.200
But basically, what I can say
is Spanner is a CP system.

00:26:29.200 --> 00:26:33.390
But the infrastructure that
supports Spanner within Google

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:36.120
has not failed us in
the way that we did not

00:26:36.120 --> 00:26:39.090
have a network partition so
far with our internal Spanner

00:26:39.090 --> 00:26:40.110
system.

00:26:40.110 --> 00:26:44.040
So the strong infrastructure
that we have within Google

00:26:44.040 --> 00:26:46.920
basically gives
us the reliability

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:49.340
and just a very
unlikely scenario

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:52.470
of a partitioning where
we would, in that case,

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:57.310
result to a consistent system
and would become unavailable.

00:26:57.310 --> 00:27:00.640
Now, for a regional
setup, we basically

00:27:00.640 --> 00:27:04.040
give an SLA for four nines,
and for a multiregional setup,

00:27:04.040 --> 00:27:09.850
we give an SLA of five nines
of availability for this.

00:27:09.850 --> 00:27:12.820
Now, if you want to get
started with Spanner,

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:17.180
the first thing that you
do is create your instance.

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:24.690
And that couldn't be easier
than doing Spanner Playground,

00:27:24.690 --> 00:27:26.070
giving it a name.

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.410
It automatically gives
you an instance ID.

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:31.260
You choose one of the four
regions that we currently

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:35.260
support, which is basically
in Asia, East and Northeast,

00:27:35.260 --> 00:27:38.910
so Taiwan and Japan, and
also Europe and Belgium,

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:41.130
and US in Iowa.

00:27:41.130 --> 00:27:43.810
So, for example, I
will choose Europe.

00:27:43.810 --> 00:27:46.810
You choose the amount of nodes
that you want to start with.

00:27:46.810 --> 00:27:52.290
You click Create, and within
seconds, you have an error.

00:27:52.290 --> 00:27:53.670
Oh.

00:27:53.670 --> 00:27:54.600
All right.

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:58.600
Let's see if I have enough
quota to have at least one node.

00:27:58.600 --> 00:27:59.100
Awesome.

00:27:59.100 --> 00:28:00.060
OK.

00:28:00.060 --> 00:28:03.000
So I didn't have enough
quota for 10 nodes,

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:07.120
but I have at least enough
quota for one node here.

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:08.730
So now you have your instance.

00:28:08.730 --> 00:28:11.590
and you basically can start
creating your database.

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:15.840
So you can choose a
normal DDL, SQL DDL.

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:18.570
Or you can use our editor
here in the console

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:20.020
to create your table.

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:24.750
Now, for the demo purpose,
I use this UI editor.

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:29.910
I'm adding some
information, primary key,

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:31.920
selecting my age
as a primary key.

00:28:31.920 --> 00:28:33.840
And I create this table.

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:37.740
And within a couple of
clicks, I have a database

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:40.410
where I can run queries
against and I can also

00:28:40.410 --> 00:28:43.090
check the schema.

00:28:43.090 --> 00:28:44.760
Now, I mentioned online updates.

00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:48.420
So I can, for instance,
add another column.

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:50.670
And I forgot the
name of the singer.

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:53.100
I want to give it a string.

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:57.730
And let's say 100
characters should be enough.

00:28:57.730 --> 00:28:59.920
I can save this.

00:28:59.920 --> 00:29:02.800
And if I reload this
page, you can see here

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:06.220
that we instantly see that this
schema update is in progress.

00:29:06.220 --> 00:29:08.390
Now, this is done
in a transaction.

00:29:08.390 --> 00:29:10.300
So basically at a
specific timestamp,

00:29:10.300 --> 00:29:12.580
you will have that
new schema available,

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:17.710
and you can accommodate
that in your application.

00:29:17.710 --> 00:29:21.250
Now, if you joined the
keynote this morning,

00:29:21.250 --> 00:29:25.040
you might have seen or are
familiar with this schema.

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:28.180
So I built a ticket
broker system,

00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:30.970
so to say, where I have a ticket
database where I'm selling

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:33.330
tickets all around the world.

00:29:33.330 --> 00:29:35.200
And what's really
important in this case

00:29:35.200 --> 00:29:38.050
is that I sell each
ticket only once.

00:29:38.050 --> 00:29:39.730
I also wanted to
have a system which

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:42.740
is as realistic as possible.

00:29:42.740 --> 00:29:44.440
So in this database,
we basically

00:29:44.440 --> 00:29:46.960
have an event table,
which shows all our events

00:29:46.960 --> 00:29:48.920
that are happening
around the world.

00:29:48.920 --> 00:29:51.800
And we have different
seating categories and venues

00:29:51.800 --> 00:29:53.290
that these events
are happening in.

00:29:53.290 --> 00:29:56.830
So you can think of a
VIP seating, a premium

00:29:56.830 --> 00:29:58.750
seating, general seating.

00:29:58.750 --> 00:30:00.760
And then once this event
is actually happening,

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:03.880
I attach, also, some
prices to these categories.

00:30:03.880 --> 00:30:07.310
So we have a ticket
category table.

00:30:07.310 --> 00:30:09.400
And at the end, we also have--

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:12.340
for each of these tickets that
are available in our system,

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:14.720
we have a ticket in
our ticket table.

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:20.800
So for the demo database that we
used today in our keynote demo,

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:25.240
we have, actually, about 2
billion rows, 2 billion tickets

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:27.440
available in the database.

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:32.590
Now, this database
runs right now live.

00:30:32.590 --> 00:30:36.070
And in this query, I want to
select all the events that

00:30:36.070 --> 00:30:40.510
are happening from now until
tomorrow evening, tomorrow

00:30:40.510 --> 00:30:41.410
night.

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:44.530
So I want to see what events
are still happening in Italy

00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:47.320
and what are the
available seats.

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:51.820
Now, when I run this query,
it will take a while.

00:30:51.820 --> 00:30:54.370
And it will give me the
result of like all the events

00:30:54.370 --> 00:30:57.020
that I have and all the
tickets that are available.

00:30:57.020 --> 00:30:58.990
And as you can see,
the seats available

00:30:58.990 --> 00:31:01.640
is a little lower than the
total seats are available.

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:05.260
So this database is actually
live, running right now.

00:31:05.260 --> 00:31:07.270
What you can do,
as well is you can

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:09.820
get an explanation, a
query plan of the query

00:31:09.820 --> 00:31:11.870
that you just executed.

00:31:11.870 --> 00:31:13.740
So what you see here
is, for instance,

00:31:13.740 --> 00:31:16.630
the CPU time that was needed.

00:31:16.630 --> 00:31:19.420
You see the rows that was
returned for this query.

00:31:19.420 --> 00:31:22.120
And you see the rows
that were scanned.

00:31:22.120 --> 00:31:25.150
Now, I'm a bit familiar
with this query

00:31:25.150 --> 00:31:26.650
because I designed it.

00:31:26.650 --> 00:31:28.870
And I see there's a lot
of rows scanned here.

00:31:28.870 --> 00:31:31.280
And I think that's way too many.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:33.400
And if I go through
my query plan,

00:31:33.400 --> 00:31:37.720
I can see that I'm doing a
table scan on my event table.

00:31:37.720 --> 00:31:39.740
Now, you might have seen it.

00:31:39.740 --> 00:31:41.260
You might have missed it.

00:31:41.260 --> 00:31:47.620
In my query, I actually had
a filter based on a timing.

00:31:47.620 --> 00:31:49.990
And it should be
able to do something

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:53.290
smarter than a table
scan on my event table.

00:31:53.290 --> 00:31:55.360
For a matter of fact,
if we look at the event

00:31:55.360 --> 00:31:57.640
table on the left
side, we're seeing

00:31:57.640 --> 00:32:02.510
that I have some indexes
defined in my table.

00:32:02.510 --> 00:32:06.565
So I talked about extensions,
SQL ANSI 2011 with extensions.

00:32:06.565 --> 00:32:09.140
So one of the things
that you can do,

00:32:09.140 --> 00:32:12.310
which you can do, also,
for many other databases,

00:32:12.310 --> 00:32:17.250
is to give the compiler, the
query compiler and optimizer,

00:32:17.250 --> 00:32:18.280
a hint.

00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:20.365
So in this case, I
want to force an index.

00:32:23.080 --> 00:32:26.380
And I have event by
date here as my index

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:30.080
and say, OK, please use
this index right now.

00:32:30.080 --> 00:32:33.370
And as we can see, the
query got a lot faster.

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:35.220
And if we go into
the explanation,

00:32:35.220 --> 00:32:39.010
I'm now down to
4,000 rows scanned

00:32:39.010 --> 00:32:41.290
instead of 400,000 rows.

00:32:41.290 --> 00:32:44.560
So you have the possibility
to also use the extension

00:32:44.560 --> 00:32:47.740
that you provide to
improve your queries

00:32:47.740 --> 00:32:48.920
and optimize your queries.

00:32:51.870 --> 00:32:54.960
Now, if you want to get
started with Cloud Spanner,

00:32:54.960 --> 00:32:56.970
one of the ways that
you can get started with

00:32:56.970 --> 00:32:59.520
is our Java client library.

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:02.010
And I kind of walked
through a small example

00:33:02.010 --> 00:33:06.430
here for our Java
client library.

00:33:06.430 --> 00:33:10.179
In this case, I'm
using a demo database.

00:33:10.179 --> 00:33:11.970
And the first thing
that I would have to do

00:33:11.970 --> 00:33:14.970
is basically get some
credentials and authentication

00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:16.110
against my database.

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:20.700
If you run that locally
with our G Cloud SDK setup,

00:33:20.700 --> 00:33:23.520
you basically live in the
context, the authentication

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:26.790
context, of your G Cloud CLI.

00:33:26.790 --> 00:33:29.310
So I'm basically just getting
these application default

00:33:29.310 --> 00:33:30.450
credentials.

00:33:30.450 --> 00:33:31.980
And the next thing
that I'm doing

00:33:31.980 --> 00:33:36.750
is setting the instance ID
that I need and the database ID

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:38.950
that I want to talk to.

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:40.960
Now, my first example
that I want to do here

00:33:40.960 --> 00:33:43.990
is I want to do a
read-only transaction.

00:33:43.990 --> 00:33:46.390
So I get a transaction context.

00:33:46.390 --> 00:33:49.225
And I execute a query.

00:33:49.225 --> 00:33:51.100
Now, this query, what
it does is it basically

00:33:51.100 --> 00:33:55.890
selects all the marquee events
that are happening today

00:33:55.890 --> 00:33:56.740
to tomorrow.

00:33:56.740 --> 00:34:00.430
So you have some
parameter placeholders,

00:34:00.430 --> 00:34:06.850
which I'm binding in my query
down here, in my statement

00:34:06.850 --> 00:34:08.872
builder.

00:34:08.872 --> 00:34:11.409
Now the last thing--

00:34:11.409 --> 00:34:14.340
as you can see, I'm already
executing the queries there.

00:34:14.340 --> 00:34:17.900
The last thing that I
want to do is already

00:34:17.900 --> 00:34:21.139
going through my results set.

00:34:21.139 --> 00:34:23.540
So I'm going to
iterate, basically,

00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:25.780
through my result
set that I'm getting.

00:34:25.780 --> 00:34:31.889
And I am fetching the results.

00:34:31.889 --> 00:34:33.980
So here I can do a get string.

00:34:33.980 --> 00:34:36.750
And I can use either
positional arguments

00:34:36.750 --> 00:34:42.139
or I can use the column
name in my example.

00:34:42.139 --> 00:34:45.250
So I'm using the positional
because I'm lazy.

00:34:45.250 --> 00:34:47.170
And then I'm executing that.

00:34:47.170 --> 00:34:49.659
And as I can see,
then hopefully,

00:34:49.659 --> 00:34:52.780
if I didn't do any mistakes,
I will see some events coming

00:34:52.780 --> 00:34:54.800
up down here, as you can see.

00:34:54.800 --> 00:34:57.230
So I have all my multievents
that are happening from today

00:34:57.230 --> 00:34:59.010
to tomorrow.

00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:02.770
Now, I talked about
time-bounded queries.

00:35:02.770 --> 00:35:06.010
So one thing that I can do
in my read-only transactions,

00:35:06.010 --> 00:35:09.290
I can actually add
timestamp bound,

00:35:09.290 --> 00:35:13.780
which sets the maximal staleness
that allow for a query.

00:35:13.780 --> 00:35:15.790
So I have to edit this here.

00:35:15.790 --> 00:35:18.820
And if I execute that
again or run this again,

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:21.760
it goes against the read
replica and can directly

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:23.470
serve that data from
the read replica

00:35:23.470 --> 00:35:27.754
without basically consulting
the leader of the Paxos crew.

00:35:27.754 --> 00:35:29.170
The next thing
that I want to show

00:35:29.170 --> 00:35:32.900
is how the write looks like.

00:35:32.900 --> 00:35:35.830
So if I have a
write-only transaction,

00:35:35.830 --> 00:35:38.930
I basically work with mutations.

00:35:38.930 --> 00:35:40.600
Now, what is a mutation?

00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:44.740
A mutation is basically
any changes in my tables.

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:48.470
And you have to look at
mutations as on cell level.

00:35:48.470 --> 00:35:52.720
So in transaction, we support
up to 20,000 mutations.

00:35:52.720 --> 00:35:54.910
If you have a table
with five columns

00:35:54.910 --> 00:35:57.580
and you load one row into
this col-- in this table,

00:35:57.580 --> 00:35:59.200
you have five mutations.

00:35:59.200 --> 00:36:02.110
If you have to find an index
on that table with another two

00:36:02.110 --> 00:36:04.810
columns, and you insert
one row, you basically

00:36:04.810 --> 00:36:07.010
have seven mutations.

00:36:07.010 --> 00:36:09.880
Now, here, in this
case, I basically

00:36:09.880 --> 00:36:12.960
add an account in my table.

00:36:12.960 --> 00:36:14.970
And I run that.

00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:23.650
If we go back to my database,
and go my demo instance,

00:36:23.650 --> 00:36:25.780
and go to my account,
and go and preview,

00:36:25.780 --> 00:36:30.070
we see that I have
inserted, here, an account.

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:32.950
Now, the next thing, if you want
to do a read/write transaction,

00:36:32.950 --> 00:36:36.630
you basically obtain
a transaction runner

00:36:36.630 --> 00:36:37.810
that we can see here.

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:43.570
So basically, we execute or
rerun a read/write transaction.

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:45.370
And through that
read/write transaction,

00:36:45.370 --> 00:36:48.850
we provide a transaction
callable from a transaction

00:36:48.850 --> 00:36:49.450
runner.

00:36:49.450 --> 00:36:51.070
So basically, it's
a function that you

00:36:51.070 --> 00:36:54.140
give to that run function.

00:36:54.140 --> 00:36:57.130
And in this run
function, you define

00:36:57.130 --> 00:37:02.000
what you want to do in terms of
reading and updating your data.

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:04.330
So in this case, I'm
selecting my account.

00:37:04.330 --> 00:37:06.700
I'm updating the
name of my account.

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:10.180
And then I'm adding these
mutations to my transaction

00:37:10.180 --> 00:37:12.190
and run this transaction.

00:37:12.190 --> 00:37:16.240
Now, why are we doing this
in a transaction runnable?

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:19.480
The reason is that our client
libraries do automatic retries

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:21.610
of transactions if they fail.

00:37:21.610 --> 00:37:24.860
So this makes it easier
for us to basically do

00:37:24.860 --> 00:37:27.370
the recovery from
failed transactions

00:37:27.370 --> 00:37:29.620
and rerun them again.

00:37:29.620 --> 00:37:36.010
So now if I go to my database
and check the preview,

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:37.960
again, you see that
I updated my name.

00:37:41.290 --> 00:37:41.790
All right.

00:37:41.790 --> 00:37:43.456
With this, I'm going
back to the slides.

00:37:48.280 --> 00:37:50.760
So the last thing that
I want to talk about

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:56.800
from the slides, once
we switch, is the--

00:37:56.800 --> 00:38:00.480
in terms of how does Spanner
fit into the storage portfolio

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:02.760
that we have on
our cloud platform,

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:06.750
so I want to just mention
a couple of things here.

00:38:06.750 --> 00:38:08.702
When do you use Spanner?

00:38:08.702 --> 00:38:12.140
I just wanted to reiterate
there are two major use

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:13.010
cases for Spanner.

00:38:13.010 --> 00:38:16.130
If you need a high
available system which

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:20.540
scales horizontally for
transactional workloads

00:38:20.540 --> 00:38:24.095
and reads and writes,
Spanner is your way to go.

00:38:24.095 --> 00:38:27.230
Even if you have a low amount
of data, you can use it.

00:38:27.230 --> 00:38:29.180
Also, if you have a
lot of data that you

00:38:29.180 --> 00:38:31.070
want to scale
basically horizontally,

00:38:31.070 --> 00:38:33.470
again, that is a way
to go for Spanner.

00:38:33.470 --> 00:38:36.680
We have a lot of other options
under the Google Cloud platform

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:38.390
portfolio.

00:38:38.390 --> 00:38:40.980
If you come from a
MySQL or Postgres world,

00:38:40.980 --> 00:38:44.150
we have a managed service
called Cloud SQL, which

00:38:44.150 --> 00:38:46.610
you can move through,
basically, as lift and shift

00:38:46.610 --> 00:38:48.470
if you want to.

00:38:48.470 --> 00:38:51.730
If you are looking at a
more NoSQL world, where

00:38:51.730 --> 00:38:54.380
you have data that
is document based,

00:38:54.380 --> 00:38:56.170
you are looking at
Cloud Datastore.

00:38:56.170 --> 00:38:59.180
Or if you have key
value based workloads,

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:02.450
which need very low latencies
for reads and writes

00:39:02.450 --> 00:39:06.240
and high scalability, you
look at Cloud Bigtable.

00:39:06.240 --> 00:39:08.750
If you have things
like blobstores,

00:39:08.750 --> 00:39:13.010
like videos, photos, things
like that, or any kind of files,

00:39:13.010 --> 00:39:15.680
you look into the
cloud storage world.

00:39:15.680 --> 00:39:20.700
And if you want to do data
analytics or relational data,

00:39:20.700 --> 00:39:24.800
so basically data warehouse
applications or scenarios,

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:28.160
you look into BigQuery, which
is our distributed and very

00:39:28.160 --> 00:39:32.725
scalable data warehouse
for relational data.

00:39:32.725 --> 00:39:35.120
With this, thank you very much.

00:39:35.120 --> 00:39:37.910
And I hope you
enjoyed the session.

00:39:37.910 --> 00:39:38.510
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:38.510 --> 00:39:40.360
Thank you.

